From: bobsixsixsix-boop <304558835+bobsixsixsix-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1042] PM / devfreq: scmi-qcom-memlat: use the Android memlat tunables on Kaanapali

The RFC driver (0126) programs every Kaanapali latency monitor with
ipm_ceil 20000000 and be_stall_floor 1. An IPM ceiling that high lets
practically every CPU vote every sample, so the DDR, LLCC and DDR_QOS votes
simply follow cpufreq (seen on the tablet: the ddr devfreq read-back tracks
the CPU clocks; a sampling window puts the vote at the 547 MHz floor 88 % of
the time only because the CPUs idle that much).

The Android BSP that ships on this device (ColorOS 17 for TB323FU) runs the
same CPUCP firmware (cpucp_a.img is loaded by the bootloader for both OSes)
with the same monitors: its "CanoeP SoC" device tree (msm-id 0x295) has the
same four DDR, three LLCC and four DDR_QOS monitors, with cpufreq->memfreq
maps and DDR/LLCC frequency tables identical to the kaanapali tables below.
It only lets CPUs vote whose instructions-per-miss is low:

 - its memlat driver (vendor_dlkm memlat.ko) programs every monitor at
   probe with ipm_ceil 400, fe/be stall floor 0, wb_pct 100, ipm filter
   25000, freq_scale 0 % with ceil/floor 5000 MHz, and sends sample_ms 8;
   its ipm_ceil sysfs store clamps to 1..500000;
 - /vendor/bin/init.kernel.post_boot-canoe_default_6_2.sh (init.kernel.post_boot.sh
   hands soc_id 660/661/704/722/723 to init.kernel.post_boot-canoe.sh, which
   picks the 6_2 variant from the 6+2 CPU topology, i.e. this tablet) then sets
   ipm_ceil: DDR gold 3000, DDR prime 13000 (plus freq_scale_pct 25 with
   floor 1500 / ceil 2800 MHz), every *latfloor monitor 300000, DDR_QOS gold
   700, DDR_QOS prime 350. LLCC monitors are not touched (400). Compute
   monitors are flagged by mon_type and ignore IPM.

Use those values on Kaanapali. The parameters the RFC driver did not send
yet (FE_STALL_FLOOR, WB_PCT, IPM_FILTER, FREQ_SCALE_PCT/CEIL/FLOOR, param ids
24 and 26-30) are already in the RFC's MEMLAT protocol list and carry the
same 12-byte {hw_type, mon_idx, val} message as IPM_CEIL; the Android driver
sends exactly these ids with 12-byte payloads per monitor (17, 23-30, 32-34,
then 31 once, then 36). They are sent only for SoCs that set mon_tunables
(Kaanapali), and a firmware error on one of them only warns: the monitor
keeps the firmware default for it instead of the probe failing and bus
scaling being lost. Glymur and Hamoa are unchanged. Compute monitors keep
ipm_ceil 0 (mon_type 1). The sample period becomes 8 ms like Android (the
devfreq polling default follows, 4 ms; the udev rule sets 1 s anyway).

One deliberate deviation: wb_filter_ipm is 0, not 25000. Android's event-map
messages are 12 bytes ({u32 num_evs, u32 hw_type, u8 cid[]}: 4 common
events inst/cyc/fe/be, 3 group events), while this driver sends 28-byte
messages with u32 cids and a fifth CONST_CYC slot. If the firmware reads the
RFC's message with Android's layout, the FE/BE/WB/ACC slots land on counter
0 (cycles) instead of "unset": the stall percentages read 100 % and wb_pct
reads 100 %, and the write-back path (wb_pct >= wb_pct_thres && ipm <=
wb_filter_ipm votes the raw CPU frequency, bypassing ipm_ceil) would let every
CPU with IPM <= 25000 vote, blunting the ceilings above. With the stall floors
at 0 and wb_filter_ipm at 0 neither path can fire on this SoC, whichever way
the firmware parses the map, so ipm_ceil is the only gate, as on Android.
The map layout itself is not changed here (not testable off-device); it is
noted for the RFC authors.

Not ported (no message for it in the RFC protocol, or not memlat):
 - the HLOS bandwidth monitors: Android's bwmon4 llcc-gold/llcc-prime
   (CPU-side, with mbps_zones, io_percent 80/120, idle_mbps 1600, sample 4 ms,
   max_freq 806000 kHz, sched_boost 1350000 kHz) and bwmon5 bwmon-ddr (LLCC->DDR,
   max_freq 3187000 kHz, idle_mbps 1600, sample 4 ms). Mainline drives the two
   CPU-side monitors with icc-bwmon (qcom,kaanapali-cpu-bwmon, a fixed 11-row
   OPP table, no sysfs tunables) and has no LLCC->DDR monitor at all;
 - adaptive_low_freq / adaptive_high_freq / adaptive_cur_freq (ids 20-22,
   defined in the RFC list but never sent by Android's boot script either),
   adaptive_level_1 (id 40, group tunable, 12 bytes), subsampling_enabled
   (id 41, 4 bytes), cpucp log level / flush (ids 3-4), hlos_cpucp_offset /
   GET_TIMESTAMP (id 38): none are set by Android at boot;
 - "boost_freq = hw_min_freq" for every bus_dcvs device (Android's qcom_dcvs
   HLOS voter, a no-op floor);
 - MEMLAT_SET_EFFECTIVE_FREQ_METHOD (39): the RFC sends 1 (legacy); Android's
   kaanapali driver never sends this id, so the firmware default applies
   there. Left as the RFC has it.
---
diff --git a/drivers/devfreq/scmi-qcom-memlat-devfreq.c b/drivers/devfreq/scmi-qcom-memlat-devfreq.c
index 9ca85b2..16786a1 100644
--- a/drivers/devfreq/scmi-qcom-memlat-devfreq.c
+++ b/drivers/devfreq/scmi-qcom-memlat-devfreq.c
@@ -98,6 +98,20 @@ enum scmi_qcom_memlat_hw_type {
  *            at or below this ceiling; a monitor with @ipm_ceil == 0 is a
  *            "compute" monitor and scales passively with cpufreq.
  * @table_len: number of valid rows in @table.
+ * @fe_stall_floor: front-end-stall threshold, in percent.
+ * @wb_pct_thres: write-back percentage at or above which a CPU may also vote
+ *                when its IPM is at or below @wb_filter_ipm.
+ * @wb_filter_ipm: IPM limit for the write-back path above.
+ * @freq_scale_pct: extra CPU frequency, in percent, added before the table
+ *                  lookup for a latency-bound CPU running between
+ *                  @freq_scale_floor_mhz and @freq_scale_ceil_mhz (0: off).
+ * @freq_scale_ceil_mhz: upper CPU frequency limit of the scaling, in MHz.
+ * @freq_scale_floor_mhz: lower CPU frequency limit of the scaling, in MHz.
+ *
+ * @fe_stall_floor, @wb_pct_thres, @wb_filter_ipm and the @freq_scale_* fields
+ * are sent (MEMLAT_FE_STALL_FLOOR, MEMLAT_WB_PCT, MEMLAT_IPM_FILTER,
+ * MEMLAT_FREQ_SCALE_*) only when the SoC's &scmi_qcom_memlat_cfg_data.mon_tunables
+ * is set; otherwise the firmware keeps its own defaults for them.
  */
 struct scmi_qcom_monitor_cfg {
 	const struct scmi_qcom_map_table *table;
@@ -106,6 +120,12 @@ struct scmi_qcom_monitor_cfg {
 	u32 cpu_mask;
 	u32 ipm_ceil;
 	u32 table_len;
+	u32 fe_stall_floor;
+	u32 wb_pct_thres;
+	u32 wb_filter_ipm;
+	u32 freq_scale_pct;
+	u32 freq_scale_ceil_mhz;
+	u32 freq_scale_floor_mhz;
 };
 
 struct scmi_qcom_memory_cfg {
@@ -123,6 +143,8 @@ struct scmi_qcom_memlat_cfg_data {
 	const struct scmi_qcom_memory_cfg *memory_cfg;
 	const u32 *common_ev;
 	bool cpucp_legacy_freq_method;
+	/* also send fe_stall_floor, wb_* and freq_scale_* for every monitor */
+	bool mon_tunables;
 	u32 cpucp_sample_ms;
 	u32 memory_cnt;
 };
@@ -763,6 +785,39 @@ static const struct scmi_qcom_memory_cfg hamoa_memory_cfg[] = {
 	},
 };
 
+/*
+ * Kaanapali per-monitor tuneables, taken from the Android BSP that ships on
+ * Kaanapali devices (same CPUCP firmware, same monitors, same cpufreq->memfreq
+ * maps and the same DDR/LLCC frequency tables as below):
+ *  - its memlat driver programs every monitor with fe/be stall floor 0,
+ *    wb_pct 100, ipm filter 25000 and freq_scale off (0 %, 5000/5000 MHz);
+ *  - its boot script then sets ipm_ceil: DDR gold 3000, prime 13000 (plus
+ *    freq_scale 25 % between 1500 and 2800 MHz), prime-latfloor 300000;
+ *    DDR_QOS gold 700, prime 350, prime-latfloor 300000; LLCC keeps the
+ *    driver default of 400;
+ *  - the memlat sample period stays at the driver default of 8 ms.
+ * Compute monitors (ipm_ceil 0 here) follow cpufreq whatever these say.
+ *
+ * wb_filter_ipm is 0 rather than Android's 25000: the write-back path
+ * (wb_pct >= wb_pct_thres && ipm <= wb_filter_ipm votes the raw CPU
+ * frequency, bypassing ipm_ceil) needs the WB and ACC counters, and neither
+ * kaanapali_*_grp_ev above nor Android's device tree wires them on this SoC.
+ * Android's 25000 is only inert as long as the firmware really sees those
+ * two slots as unset; 0 keeps the path inert whatever the firmware computes
+ * for wb_pct (ipm is >= 1 for any CPU that retired instructions), so
+ * ipm_ceil is the only gate, exactly as on Android.
+ */
+#define KAANAPALI_MON_TUNABLES(_pct, _floor_mhz, _ceil_mhz)	\
+	.fe_stall_floor = 0,					\
+	.be_stall_floor = 0,					\
+	.wb_pct_thres = 100,					\
+	.wb_filter_ipm = 0,					\
+	.freq_scale_pct = (_pct),				\
+	.freq_scale_floor_mhz = (_floor_mhz),			\
+	.freq_scale_ceil_mhz = (_ceil_mhz)
+
+#define KAANAPALI_MON_DEFAULTS	KAANAPALI_MON_TUNABLES(0, 5000, 5000)
+
 static const struct scmi_qcom_memory_cfg kaanapali_memory_cfg[] = {
 	{
 		.memory_type = MEMLAT_HW_DDR,
@@ -774,10 +829,11 @@ static const struct scmi_qcom_memory_cfg kaanapali_memory_cfg[] = {
 		.memory_range = { .min_freq_khz = 547000, .max_freq_khz = 5333000 },
 		.monitor_cfg = (const struct scmi_qcom_monitor_cfg[]) {
 			{
+				/* Android "gold" */
 				.name = "mon_0",
 				.cpu_mask = 0x3f,
-				.ipm_ceil = 20000000,
-				.be_stall_floor = 1,
+				.ipm_ceil = 3000,
+				KAANAPALI_MON_DEFAULTS,
 				.table_len = 6,
 				.table = (const struct scmi_qcom_map_table[]) {
 					{ .cpu_freq_mhz = 787, .mem_freq_mhz = 547 },
@@ -789,10 +845,11 @@ static const struct scmi_qcom_memory_cfg kaanapali_memory_cfg[] = {
 				}
 			},
 			{
+				/* Android "prime" */
 				.name = "mon_1",
 				.cpu_mask = 0xc0,
-				.ipm_ceil = 20000000,
-				.be_stall_floor = 1,
+				.ipm_ceil = 13000,
+				KAANAPALI_MON_TUNABLES(25, 1500, 2800),
 				.table_len = 8,
 				.table = (const struct scmi_qcom_map_table[]) {
 					{ .cpu_freq_mhz = 614, .mem_freq_mhz = 547 },
@@ -806,8 +863,10 @@ static const struct scmi_qcom_memory_cfg kaanapali_memory_cfg[] = {
 				}
 			},
 			{
+				/* Android "gold-compute" */
 				.name = "mon_2",
 				.cpu_mask = 0xff,
+				KAANAPALI_MON_DEFAULTS,
 				.table_len = 2,
 				.table = (const struct scmi_qcom_map_table[]) {
 					{ .cpu_freq_mhz = 2035, .mem_freq_mhz = 547 },
@@ -815,10 +874,11 @@ static const struct scmi_qcom_memory_cfg kaanapali_memory_cfg[] = {
 				}
 			},
 			{
+				/* Android "prime-latfloor" */
 				.name = "mon_3",
 				.cpu_mask = 0xc0,
-				.ipm_ceil = 20000000,
-				.be_stall_floor = 1,
+				.ipm_ceil = 300000,
+				KAANAPALI_MON_DEFAULTS,
 				.table_len = 2,
 				.table = (const struct scmi_qcom_map_table[]) {
 					{ .cpu_freq_mhz = 2726, .mem_freq_mhz = 547 },
@@ -837,10 +897,11 @@ static const struct scmi_qcom_memory_cfg kaanapali_memory_cfg[] = {
 		.memory_range = { .min_freq_khz = 282000, .max_freq_khz = 1350000 },
 		.monitor_cfg = (const struct scmi_qcom_monitor_cfg[]) {
 			{
